\mt1 Psalms

\c 11

\d For the lead player. Of David.

\s1 In the LORD I Take Refuge

\b
\q1
\v 1 In the LORD I take refuge.\f + \fr 11:1 \fk KONTEXT \fq take refuge \ft The Hebrew verb *hasah* does not mean "trust" in the abstract. It means to take physical shelter — a person crouching behind a wall, a bird pressing into a rock crevice. The word carries the body into the theology. To say "In the LORD I take refuge" is not a mental state but a bodily act of staying — refusing to flee the public square when the powerful say the situation is hopeless. The psalm's opening is a political act as much as a spiritual one: the psalmist chooses not to run.\f*
\q1 How can you say to me:
\q1 Fly away like a sparrow to the hills?\f + \fr 11:1 \fk KULTUR \fq sparrow \ft In the Hebrew Bible the sparrow (*tsippor*) is not charming. It is the smallest, most ordinary, most hunted bird — vulnerability personified. The advisers say: accept your powerlessness and flee. But in Psalm 84:3 the same sparrow "finds a house" near God's altar. The advisers' image of helplessness becomes, in the Psalter's memory, an image of shelter. — The quotation marks end at v.3. Everything between is the advisers' speech — voices urging flight. Notice that YHWH's name never appears in their words. The theological absence is their fundamental problem: they have assessed the situation without reference to God. The psalmist's counter-statement (vv.4-7) says "the LORD" four times in two verses. The psalm argues theologically through structure before it argues through statement.\f*

\b
\q1
\v 2 Look — the wicked bend their bows.
\q1 They fit the arrow to the string
\q2 to shoot from the dark\f + \fr 11:2 \fk KULTUR \fq from the dark \ft Shooting from darkness is not only deadly but dishonorable. In the ancient world, honorable combat was face-to-face; assassination from concealment was the coward's method. It doubled the injustice: the victim is killed AND shamed by the manner of the killing. — The contrast with v.4 is deliberate: the hidden archers act where they think no one sees. But YHWH's eyes "take in everything" — the divine gaze penetrates the very darkness the archers depend on.\f*
\q2 at the upright.

\b
\q1
\v 3 The foundations are torn down —\f + \fr 11:3 \fk TEXT \fq foundations \ft The Hebrew word *shatot* (from the root *yasad*, "to lay a foundation") appears only here in the entire Hebrew Bible — a hapax legomenon. Its uniqueness means the ambiguity is built into the word itself: these could be the foundations of social order worth defending, or the foundations of an unjust system whose collapse may itself be justice. The psalm refuses to specify whose foundations or whether their destruction is catastrophe or liberation. Liberation readers ask the question the psalm leaves open: when the powerful say "the foundations are being destroyed," whose foundations do they mean? — The advisers' speech ends here with a question that assumes helplessness. The psalmist answers not with argument but with declaration — vv.4-7 simply state what is true about YHWH.\f*
\q1 what can the righteous do?

\s1 The LORD Sees

\b
\q1
\v 4 The LORD is in his holy place.\f + \fr 11:4 \fk KONTEXT \fq holy place ... heavens \ft The dual structure — YHWH in the earthly sanctuary AND on the heavenly throne — is not contradiction but a claim about the scope of divine sovereignty. In ancient Near Eastern temple theology, the earthly shrine and the heavenly dwelling were understood as linked: God simultaneously local and cosmic, present in the community's worship space and sovereign over all creation. The psalm's point: YHWH is not absent (as the advisers implied), not merely in heaven out of reach, not merely limited to a building — but both at once. Wherever the archers hide, the divine gaze reaches.\f*
\q1 The LORD — his throne is in the heavens.
\q1 His eyes take in everything.
\q1 His gaze scrutinizes every person alive.\f + \fr 11:4 \fk TEXT \fq his gaze scrutinizes \ft The Hebrew *ap'appayim yibhanu* — literally "his eyelids examine" — cannot be translated literally in English (as Robert Alter notes, "in English one cannot see with the eyelids"). The word names a narrowed, forensic gaze: the searching look of an assayer testing ore, not the general oversight of a watchman. The verb *bahan* (scrutinize/assay) carries metallurgical connotation — the precision of fire applied to metal to reveal what it truly is. — This divine seeing is not surveillance of the vulnerable. It is exposure of the powerful who act in darkness. YHWH's gaze is directed at the archers of v.2 — advocacy for those courts refuse to see, not a cosmic police state.\f*

\b
\q1
\v 5 The LORD scrutinizes the righteous
\q1 and those who love violence —\f + \fr 11:5 \fk KONTEXT \fq those who love violence \ft The Hebrew *ohev hamas* — "lover of violence" — names not a one-time act but a character orientation: these are people who are drawn to violence, who practice it as a way of being in the world. The word *hamas* is raw, relational violence (not abstract "evil") — the kind that breaks bodies and communities. — What follows is extraordinary: "with all his being, he opposes them." The Hebrew *nafsho* (his *nefesh*) means YHWH's entire self — gut, life, being — stands against those who love violence. This is not merely a divine policy decision. It is visceral divine opposition, total and personal.\f*
\q1 with all his being, he opposes them.

\b
\q1
\v 6 He rains down on them\f + \fr 11:6 \fk TEXT \fq coals of fire, sulfur \ft The Hebrew text has a likely scribal error here: the consonants of *pahim* ("snares/traps") are almost certainly a transposition of *pehamey* ("coals of") — a single letter-swap that ancient manuscripts preserve in both forms. The corrected reading produces a coherent theophanic judgment sequence: coals, fire, sulfur, scorching wind. — The language is the language of Sodom (Genesis 19:24). The psalmist reaches for the most extreme act of divine judgment in Genesis to make a single point: God has done this before. When human courts failed and collective violence went unchecked, YHWH acted. The allusion is not decorative but structural — proof of prior willingness. — "What fills their cup" = the portion allocated to the wicked. In the ancient world, the cup was the image for one's appointed share — whether blessing or judgment. Here: the wicked's cup is full of fire.\f*
\q1 coals of fire, sulfur,
\q1 a scorching wind.
\q1 This is what fills their cup.

\b
\q1
\v 7 The LORD is righteous.\f + \fr 11:7 \fk KONTEXT \fq the upright will see his face \ft This is the psalm's climax and its most intimate moment. "Seeing God's face" in the ancient world meant receiving full audience with a king — recognition, favor, vindication. In the Psalter it becomes a recurring promise of the deepest possible encounter with the divine (see also Psalms 17:15, 27:4, 73:28). — Notice the inclusio: the psalm began with "In the LORD I take refuge" (v.1) and ends with "the upright will see his face" (v.7). The refuge-seeker becomes the face-seer. The shelter becomes encounter. The psalm promises not safety but presence — not that the danger will disappear, but that the one who stays will see God. — The Hebrew text reads "their face" (*paneymo*), which has no clear antecedent. Most modern commentators follow ancient manuscript variants that read "his face" (*panayw*) — YHWH's face. This is the reading followed here.\f*
\q1 He loves what is just.
\q1 The upright will see his face.
